00:00:00.790 --> 00:00:11.030 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  we're going into the third wave already  very very exhausted and tired we are  losing staff and that's going to be the  biggest challenge  [Applause]  you can have capacity what if you don't  have nurses  what if you don't have doctors this is  the scene  inside toronto's humber river hospital  in the throes of the third wave  of the pandemic icu beds are full  patients are younger and sicker and the  staff  are being pushed to their limits i'm  very concerned where we're headed our  capacity to  continue on like this the pandemic has  taken such a toll on health care workers  that some are leaving the profession  they once loved and dedicated their  lives to  and as cases of coven 19 swell again  straining hospitals across the country  the staff that are left are tired and  struggling to keep up  at the beginning of the pandemic  everybody was ready and willing i  remember getting so many emails saying  hey i'm ready to come in i'm ready to  come and help  i don't get any emails like that anymore  dr nisha bakshi is the internal medicine  lead at the royal alexandra hospital in  edmonton  she's also in charge of the hospital's  kovud response  everybody's tired of it it's exhausting  to work on the kova unit  you are there for a 10 hour shift you  know you're wearing your full ppe  right now i think that we're seeing so  many young patients come in  that it's also there's some moral injury  and emotional burnout  so just finishing up um shift today on  the kovid unit  we're starting to see our  hospitalizations climb steadily  we're tired we're battle worn but we  will continue to show up  dr bakshi actually holds the record for  the most days spent on the covid ward at  her hospital  it's come at a cost it's come at a  mental health cost as a physical cost  within the medical field and  particularly with physicians  i think it's still viewed as a sign of  weakness that if i admit that i'm  depressed  or anxious or have ptsd it must mean  that i cannot do my job  in january dr bakshi who's also a mother  of two young children  was diagnosed with post-traumatic stress  disorder or ptsd  it's a mental health condition that can  occur after a person  experiences or witnesses a traumatic  event and so december i describe it as  chaos  it was it was just chaos there was new  patients  all the time there were patients  crashing all the time  and i noticed that i was having  difficulty sleeping i was having a lot  of nightmares  i was reliving and replaying  conversations i had with families i  think that was probably the  the biggest thing that hit me i didn't  care if i showed up or not  and i knew that that was not like me but  i think what really did it was my  husband my husband said to me he said  you're not yourself  you know you're you're angry you're  taking things out on us  and i know that you've been through a  lot and maybe it's time to get some help  and and that was it she got help and has  been seeing a counselor  since i'm a pretty resilient person i  have i think  fantastic coping skills but even myself  i was tested i was put at a limit that i  couldn't  sustain and it's not just physicians who  are experiencing this  the vast majority of all healthcare  workers reported worsening mental health  during the pandemic  according to statistics canada and among  workers who had direct contact with  patients who may be sick with kovid  that number is even higher there's  always a nurse there and that's pretty  unique to our profession like 24  7 day and night 365 if you're in a  hospital  a nurse is there nurses provide the bulk  and the majority of patient care  denika mcpherson has worked as an icu  nurse in toronto for eight years  right now she's treating patients with  covet 19  while also studying to become a nurse  practitioner as you step into a covered  positive room i can't  i don't even know how to describe that  to somebody you just have this  like silent prayer that you you throw up  to the god and you're like i hope this  ppe is work on properly and working  and then you just walk in that room and  you just have to trust that everything  is fine  even before the pandemic nurse's stress  levels and workloads were high  a 2019 canadian study found that nurses  experience widespread and severe  symptoms of ptsd  anxiety depression burnout and suicidal  behavior  at a rate higher than the general  population and now  the pandemic has exacerbated things  workloads have increased  and nurses are being pushed to their  breaking point you feel like  tired and depleted and you're just  wondering when the end is coming like is  it going to come  brigade umagba is also an icu nurse  she says she's exhausted and burnt out  and it's starting to impact her  relationship with her nine-year-old  daughter  i just feel this weakness and um a lot  of times i can't even play with her  like i'm tired i need to recover my feet  hurts my legs hurt my muscles hurt my  neck hurts so everywhere hurts  it's more it's more like a chronic  situation at this point  but i'm just really mentally drained  berget says a month ago  she started having nightmares and  trouble sleeping  and she says it's not just her her  colleagues are experiencing  the same thing the amount of dead people  we see right and also  the cold blues the chaos is really  affecting a lot of people  she says more needs to be done for  nurses reduced workloads  counseling increasing pay and wages and  stronger public health measures  would give nurses incentives to stay we  we also  think that this could have been avoided  so with all of that it's just put so  much burden on us  and and people are asking what's the  point  the turnover of nurses was a major  problem before the pandemic  it's estimated one in five nurses left  the profession  every year and the pandemic has  accelerated this  we we joke and say we wish we could  split ourselves into like two or three  people to get our work done but it's  it's not possible  it's very hard to stay in a profession  where you have very hard workloads and  you're  feeling that ethical or moral distress  that you're not  able to deliver optimal or the best care  and as hospitals across the country ramp  up their capacity  adding new icu beds to deal with the  latest surge of covet cases  some provinces like hard hit ontario are  searching for thousands of nurses  there's also a shortage of personal  support workers  it's rampant right now a lot of psw's  are leaving the field  a field that we once loved and dedicated  our lives to  sharon tanner clarkson has been a psw  for nearly a decade  she works in palliative care in toronto  she kept going to work  amid covet outbreaks and scares  colleagues getting sick  daily stress and constant rule changes  she showed up until she couldn't she's  now off on stress leave without pay  it's been really emotional there's been  days where you spend time  in your car at lunch just to get your  mask off  and you're crying because you're totally  frustrated was there a final straw for  you  a moment where you thought i just can't  do it anymore  there was my last day  before i went off on my leave and i  felt like the walls were caving in we  were  short staffed i left in tears that day  absolute tears bawling and just walked  right out you know i've pretty broad  shoulders i've been doing  this type of work for a very long time  but this whole  pandemic and this whole situation has  just really  broke me do you think you'll go back to  work sadly  um i may not go back  to what i was once doing and loving  where are you seeing the most shortages  everywhere  if we don't really address health human  resource situation  our system is going to fall apart  abby sriharin is a professor at the  university of toronto  who studies burnout in the medical field  so the studies  pre-pandemic have shown 50 to 60 percent  of  health workforce was experiencing  burnout but the pandemic has actually  increased that rate although burnout  itself is not technically a medical  condition  it can cause depression anxiety and  fatigue  which can lead to feelings of numbness  anger and guilt  physically it can cause high cholesterol  diabetes heart disease  and various other illnesses that can  shorten a person's lifespan  so people go on stress leaves right and  you are now not in that environment  that's triggering the stress and burnout  so you will feel better you go back and  it's going to happen again  unless we address the working conditions  and how  our people are compensated and what type  of  incentives are there to keep them in the  workforce  we are not going to see any changes  in every facet of canada's healthcare  system people are leaving their jobs  others are sticking it out until the end  of the pandemic out of loyalty for their  colleagues and patients  but as soon as it's over some say  they'll also walk away  the fear is there could be a mass exodus  among canada's healthcare workers  impacting the entire system for years to  come  how worried are you about hospitals  capacity to treat people during the  third wave  and into the future acutely right now  i'm very worried and i don't think we're  going to be able to ignore all the  non-cloven patients coming in they're  very sick they're going to need care  and that's going to translate into a  system that cannot keep up  it's been more than a year of showing up  when they were called  working long hours in ppe until their  whole body ate  setting up video calls so families can  say final goodbyes  they cried with them slept a few hours  and did it all over again  and now with no end in sight how will  they manage this punishing third wave  it's no secret that healthcare workers  are resilient does that make you hopeful  it does it also makes me feel sad  because i think there's an expectation  that we are resilient  and it comes back to that stigma of  mental health  in medicine that you know this is what  we were trained for we deal with life  and death but  i think that there there is a limit to  how much we should be expecting from  healthcare workers  because right now i can tell you that  most people are not resilient  most people are still just surviving i  think resiliency is probably at an  all-time low

